WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.880
Ah, the Super Bowl commercial.
The pinnacle of artistic expression.

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:07.360
When done well, they challenge the mind, inspire the heart,

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:12.340
and some - only the greatest of them all - can truly evolve us as a culture.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:13.800
(Wasuuuuuuuuup!)

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:14.420
(Wasuuuuuuuuup!)

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:14.920
(Wasuuuuuuuuup!)

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:15.880
(Bleeeeeeeeeehhhhh!)

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:18.740
Hey, look, I didn't say it evolved us for the better.

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:24.840
[Film Theory intro theme]

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:38.220
Hello Internet! welcome to Film Theory,

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:41.560
Where we always root, root, root for the home team,

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:44.040
as long as that team's mascot is statistical analysis.

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:50.220
And before we begin, a quick shoutout to Merginq - the offical first comment on the Poppy conspiracy video.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:54.340
To be that early, you either rang the subscription bell, or you're part of the Illuminati.

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:56.480
If it's the first, hey, thanks for the support.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:58.680
If it's the second, please don't make me disappear.

00:00:58.740 --> 00:00:59.460
Please.

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:01.560
I HAVE SO MANY THEORIES TO GIVE!

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:05.380
But enough groveling to the Illuminati. We're at that time of the year, dear Theorists

00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:10.220
when the national conversation is dominated for an entire week by a 4-hour marathon

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:13.980
of grown men running into each other to give the most aggressive of hugs.

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:15.820
To answer the age-old question:

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:17.740
(♫ Are you ready for some FOOTBALL?! ♫)

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:19.240
No. No, I am not.

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:25.200
And no, Monday Night Football, no amount of destroying national landmarks is gonna get me ready. Thanks though.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:28.980
Now all of this isn't to say that I don't like the Super Bowl -  far from it, actually.

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:32.640
True, I might not be the most knowledgeable person when it comes to Sportsball,

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:35.120
but I do love gargantuan plates of chicken wings,

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:38.280
and snarkily tweeting at halftime shows that go hilariously awry!

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:41.140
No, not that one. Naw, THIS one.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:47.480
You do you, left shark!  You do you.
DANCE! DANCE LIKE NOBODY'S WATCHING!

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:51.900
Nope, throughout the evening, away from the pigskin, there's another higher stakes game being played

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:55.300
that I'm more interested in. And no I'm not talking about the Kitten Bowl,

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:56.840
I'm talking about the commercials.

00:01:56.840 --> 00:02:02.100
Almost one in four Super Bowl viewers last year reported that they were watching purely for the commercials.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:03.180
Which is fascinating.

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:06.820
I mean, everything from Tivo to ad blockers to YouTube Red

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:09.620
seems to indicate that we're trying really hard to avoid commercials,

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:12.640
but one day out of the year, we all agree to give 'em a chance.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:17.620
And companies pay - 5 million dollars for just 30 seconds!

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:22.160
In the next 10 years that number is expected to climb to TEN million dollars for 30 seconds.

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:28.480
At that rate, someone screaming "DIET COKE!" at the camera for one second would cost $150,000.

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:31.360
One hundred fifty THOUSAND for a SECOND!

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:36.560
but sometimes if you do it right, you create a classic, like the Apple 1984 commercial,

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:38.800
or the Wendy's "Where's the Beef?" ad.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:40.880
Note, though, that I said, "if you do it right."

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:46.080
Because for every memorable one, there are dozens that are forgotten faster than an American Idol runner-up.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:48.960
Except for you, Clay Aiken, you'll never be invisible to me.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:53.780
So today I'm setting out to determine, based on the data, what makes the perfect Super Bowl commercial.

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:57.440
I measured various ad scoring systems, best and worst of the year lists,

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:01.440
fluctuations in corporate stock prices, Google Trends search data,

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:05.100
Basically everything about the commercials from the past ten Superbowls

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:06.980
to answer the ultimate question;

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:09.740
Who will win the BIGGEST of big games?

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:12.620
The game for Superbowl Commerical Supremacy.

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:16.640
So strap on your helmets, theorist team, and pat the butt next to you

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:18.400
because it's time to kick off!

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:20.720
That's a footballing term for starting the game.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.020
Now if we're going to build the perfect Superbowl commercial,

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:24.480
it's a good idea to start simple.

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:25.980
What should the commercial sell?

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:28.020
The obvious answer seems to be beer.

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:31.980
Since beer commercials dominate the list of the highest rated ads over the past ten years,

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:34.260
with twenty-three of the top hundred ads,

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:36.680
the most of any other product category,

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:41.260
and an average of USA Today Ad Meter score of 7.91 out of 10.

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:45.460
That Ad Meter, by the way, is the most famous reviewing mechanism for Superbowl commercials,

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:48.580
And a score of 7.91 means that the audience liked those commercials a lot.

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:50.940
So long story short, just make a beer commercial, right?

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:58.420
Well, those statistics should come with an asterisk, because every one of those 23 commercials is for either Budweiser or Bud Light,

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:04.300
and Anheiser Bush, which makes both of those beers, has multiple ads running during the ol' big game.

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:10.640
For the past two years, sure, they've had two commercials in the top ten, but four MORE that didn't do NEARLY as well.

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:13.840
So just being a beer isn't gonna result in a slam goal.

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:15.140
Uh, a field down.

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:18.300
Just kidding, I know enough about sports to know that it's called a home run.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:20.120
In fact, being a beer might be the football equivalent of

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:21.920
being put in the penalty box.

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:25.340
In a study at Stanford's graduate school of business around Super Bowl commercials,

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:28.900
researchers found that when two major competing brands advertise,

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:31.720
most of the gains of running the ad are eroded.

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:34.160
So, in other words, even if you ran a great beer ad,

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:37.180
There's a high likelihood that Bud Light 
 would get the credit for it anyway.

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:39.360
Instead, you'll make the most money off your commercial

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:41.540
if nobody's selling a product in direct competition with yours,

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:45.180
eliminating beers, cars, and insurance providers.

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:47.660
Let me just say, I know that football has a lot of head injuries,

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:50.780
but, really, WHY are there so many insurance commercials during the Super Bowl?

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:52.880
Because I, as a football viewer,

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:55.440
want to think about responsible economic decisions,

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:59.300
while my wing-stained fingers reach for another handful of pork rinds?

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:01.300
Speaking of, though, snack food.

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:05.620
It's actually the best product to advertise during the Super Bowl for multiple reasons.

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:09.140
One, it's easy to differentiate Doritos chips from Dannon yogurt.

00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:13.020
One is delicious, and one is bacteria-infested mil-[laughing]

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:16.600
I have never understood you, yogurt. You are so strange. [beep noise]

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:20.040
One is delicious, and one is bacteria-infested mil-[laughing]

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:20.960
I can't do it. [beep noise]

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:24.680
One is delicious, and one- [laughing] God damn it! Get through it, Mat! [beep noise]

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:27.680
One is delicious, and one is bacteria-infested milk.

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:29.800
And two, according to the same Stanford study,

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:33.300
if a Super Bowl commercial can draw an association between the consumption of its product

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:38.080
and watching sports, well then, people are more likely to buy it in future sports-watching sessions.

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:41.900
So, okay, we're hockin' snack food, but what's the optimal time to do it?

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:46.960
According to the data, you don't want to come too early - ...phrasing... but also don't want to risk running too late.

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:51.820
Airing a commercial later in the game runs the risk of missing viewers who have turned the game off

00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:53.560
because the score isn't close.

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:58.840
In 2014, for example, the Seattle Seahawks demolished the Denver Broncos 43 to 8.

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:01.680
And by the fourth quarter, the result was a forgone conclusion.

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:06.580
As a result, at the end of the game, there were only about 46 million viewers still left watching.

00:06:06.580 --> 00:06:09.220
As such, the second quarter is ideal.

00:06:09.220 --> 00:06:12.880
Not only are the overall rankings of commercials highest during this quarter,

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:16.640
but putting your ad in the second quarter also capitalizes on another benefit,

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:18.240
social media traffic.

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:22.000
This Nielsen graph shows the distribution of tweets during last year's Super Bowl,

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:26.680
and that spike in the middle shows that people are much more active on Twitter during halftime.

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:31.120
Airing a commercial during the second quarter increases the likelihood that any social media traffic

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:35.820
will be fresh on people's Facebook and Twitter feeds before everything gets buried under snarky Tweets

00:06:35.820 --> 00:06:39.460
about the halftime show, thereby getting it more views and more shares.

00:06:39.460 --> 00:06:43.360
And seriously, let's face it, when are you most in the mood for a snack food commercial?

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:46.580
Early on, when somebody might actually go on a snack run during halftime?

00:06:46.580 --> 00:06:50.200
Or in the fourth quarter, when you've already eaten half your body weight in nachos?

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:52.440
I mean, even FatPat has his limits.

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:55.900
So we have the topic and the timing, now all we need is the tone.

00:06:55.900 --> 00:06:57.780
What style of commercial works the best?

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:02.440
Is it better to go funny, like Betty-White-talking-trash-during-a-game-of-pickup-football-funny,

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:04.060
(Betty: That's not what your girlfriend said!)

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:08.420
Or do you smack 'em right in the feels, like, say, a lost puppy who's saved by some horses

00:07:08.420 --> 00:07:11.800
and then they become friends, and then the puppy is reunited with his owner?

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:14.860
And... why are we chopping onions in my recording closet?

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:15.900
[sniff] I'm okay.

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:18.580
Well, here's the big secret - it doesn't matter.

00:07:18.580 --> 00:07:21.280
If you truly want to make a winning Super Bowl commercial,

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:23.280
just feature an animal.

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:26.260
No joke, nothing beats the power of cute.

00:07:26.260 --> 00:07:32.160
Throw in a sad puppy or a funny herd of cats, and overall Ad Meter audience approval scores for those commercials

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:34.100
leap up to an average of 8.

00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:39.420
A full one and a half points above a typical ad spot without animals, regardless of the tone.

00:07:39.420 --> 00:07:42.780
Just, uh, make sure that you don't go overboard and create something like

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:46.400
Mountain Dew's puppy-baby-monkey monstrosity of 2016.

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:50.640
Just.... just no. It's like an Undertale amalgamate of awful.

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:57.920
There's a reason this pile of brand-approved barf reviewed at a 3.9/10. Abysmal!

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:02.620
But then again, looking at the data, it did create the biggest internet response of the night,

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:05.840
with almost 50,000 tweets immediately after it aired.

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:08.900
So is the best strategy to make the worst commercial?

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:13.300
Well, no, actually. Looking at the stock price for Pepsico, the company which owns Mountain Dew,

00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:18.620
it didn't do a lot for business the next day, only getting a slight boost of 1.17 points.

00:08:18.620 --> 00:08:22.700
Any marginal benefit they got from the Super Bowl commercial died away within the week.

00:08:22.700 --> 00:08:27.780
And actually, when you come to look at the data, it's a common trend with companies that advertise during the Super Bowl.

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:31.840
Looking at Google Trends search data, sure, the day of the game and immediately after,

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:35.320
searches for Doritos, the commercial winner of the last few years,

00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:40.200
shows a big spike in search, but by the end of the week, they're back down to normal levels.

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:44.560
People have moved on with their lives and think about Doritos the same way they've always thought about Doritos.

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:46.800
As triangular-shaped signs of the Illuminati.

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:51.080
And that, quite honestly, leads us to our ultimate answer. The Illuminati.

00:08:51.080 --> 00:08:52.380
Nope, nope, sorry, that was last video.

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:57.120
The ultimate answer to how to create the best Super Bowl commercial is,

00:08:57.120 --> 00:08:58.100
you don't.

00:08:58.100 --> 00:09:00.300
Sure, you can shell out your five million dollars,

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:02.780
grab your snack food, pin down a few cute animals,

00:09:02.780 --> 00:09:05.980
shove something funny into the second half, and then cross your fingers

00:09:05.980 --> 00:09:11.660
that the masses A) are watching, B) don't hate it, and C), no one else is shillin' for a similar product,

00:09:11.660 --> 00:09:14.440
or, you just spend that 5 million dollars somewhere else.

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:18.980
Somewhere better. Somewhere where you will actually get a solid return on that money.

00:09:18.980 --> 00:09:24.860
Because here's the thing - making a Super Bowl spot is the biggest gamble of the game.

00:09:24.860 --> 00:09:29.640
Competition amongst advertisers is the fiercest of the year, not everyone watches the commercials,

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:34.240
but most of all, the value that you get out of it is questionable at best.

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:37.420
Remember that I mentioned before that Bud Light is usually a top contender of this thing?

00:09:37.420 --> 00:09:41.620
In fact, Bud Light has been the official beer of the NFL since 2011,

00:09:41.620 --> 00:09:45.820
and paid 1.2 billion dollars for that right,

00:09:45.820 --> 00:09:49.840
but in that time, its volumes have fallen more than ten percent,

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:52.880
as they lose ground to independent local craft beers.

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:58.000
Sure, based on Google Trends, brands like Doritos get a strong bump in search every year that they run an ad,

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:00.000
but it goes away in a few days.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:04.780
In fact, one ad research firm estimates that 80% of Super Bowl commercials

00:10:04.780 --> 00:10:06.740
don't boost sales.

00:10:06.740 --> 00:10:10.360
Another survey by the marketing analytics agency Adlucent showed that

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:14.300
less than 1% of viewers watch to influence any kind of purchasing.

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:20.000
And looking back at history, plenty of companies that have run Super Bowl ads have gone out of business shortly thereafter.

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:26.040
One of the most famous was pets.com, a company whose first commercial was a Super Bowl ad,

00:10:26.040 --> 00:10:32.560
that was ranked #1 of the year and had the highest brand recall by audiences of any other ad.

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:37.680
And yet, despite building a bunch of brand recognition by spending over 11 million dollars on ads,

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:42.060
that year, they only earned 619 thousand dollars worth of business.

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:46.880
Even the best ad of the Bowl couldn't bring in customers, and they were quickly bankrupt.

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:51.260
Then there was Just for Feet, which went out of business in large part thanks to their racist ad

00:10:51.260 --> 00:10:53.740
of drugging a Kenyan runner to give him tennis shoes.

00:10:53.740 --> 00:10:56.440
Or, how about Wix? A company that never posted a profit,

00:10:56.440 --> 00:11:00.440
but decided to run a ten million dollar ad anyway in the hopes of turning their fortune?

00:11:00.440 --> 00:11:03.040
For the next two days, their stock plummeted!

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:06.500
Not only because they didn't get new customers, but because the stockholders were like

00:11:06.500 --> 00:11:08.160
WTF are you doing?!

00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:11.200
Lucky for them, the stock price returned to average later in the week.

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:15.220
Even going all the way back to that iconic Apple commercial I mentioned before,

00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:20.200
this was perhaps the commercial that made the Super Bowl ads into the spectacle that they are today.

00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:25.320
Oh, and Apple tried to do it again next year, with an ad spot showing people jumping off a cliff like lemmings,

00:11:25.320 --> 00:11:31.860
it didn't go over so well, and they wouldn't do another ad during the big game for another 14 years.

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:35.220
But perhaps most telling of all is Esurance last year,

00:11:35.220 --> 00:11:39.940
a company who didn't run an ad during the game, instead doing it during the pregame,

00:11:39.940 --> 00:11:42.100
and they dominated social media,

00:11:42.100 --> 00:11:46.460
getting eight times the amount of mentions of their hashtag relative to second place.

00:11:46.460 --> 00:11:48.620
And those are just a few select examples!

00:11:48.620 --> 00:11:51.280
So instead, what do you do with that five million dollar money pile?

00:11:51.280 --> 00:11:53.100
Shove it into your mattress? Nah.

00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:56.780
Buy 1.7 million Crunchwrap Supremes? Nyeh, not that hungry.

00:11:56.780 --> 00:12:01.100
Spend it on YouTube? Absolutely yes. That one.

00:12:01.100 --> 00:12:04.880
And sure, you might think I'm playing favorites, because, y'know, this is where I work and all,

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:08.240
but let's compare. The main value everyone cites about the Super Bowl

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:12.280
is that it gets your ad in front of 100+ million people who watch the game.

00:12:12.280 --> 00:12:15.580
Okay, well, for five million dollars, using YouTube's ad-buying tools,

00:12:15.580 --> 00:12:19.940
you could pay to have your ad show up in front of at least 100 million video playbacks.

00:12:19.940 --> 00:12:21.680
Seriously, that's about what the cost would be.

00:12:21.680 --> 00:12:23.680
And that's assuming five cents per view,

00:12:23.680 --> 00:12:25.820
which would be what's called a "targeted view".

00:12:25.820 --> 00:12:29.040
You see, by paying a bit more, I, as an ad-buying executive,

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:33.760
can make sure that my ad runs in front of certain videos in order to target certain types of viewers.

00:12:33.760 --> 00:12:37.040
It wouldn't make much sense to run an engine oil ad in front of Zoella,

00:12:37.040 --> 00:12:39.400
since her audience isn't tuning up the car that much.

00:12:39.400 --> 00:12:42.400
But if I really wanted to, I could, by paying a bit more.

00:12:42.400 --> 00:12:45.580
And for five cents, you can actually get a fair amount of targeting.

00:12:45.580 --> 00:12:50.100
In other words, for five cents a view, I could force my freakish Mountain Dew puppy-monkey-baby

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:53.460
in front of all gamers, and also everyone doing a cringe compilation,

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:58.340
and boom! Suddenly I've found my perfect audience for that weird freakshow I thought was a good idea

00:12:58.340 --> 00:13:00.620
that clearly won't sell me any more Mountain Dew Jumpstart,

00:13:00.620 --> 00:13:04.780
but hey, at least the snarky gamers will be making fun of it and I'll be all the trending hashtags.

00:13:04.780 --> 00:13:07.840
But not only would I have more control over who would be watching my video,

00:13:07.840 --> 00:13:11.520
I'd be more sure that those people WOULD be watching my video.

00:13:11.520 --> 00:13:14.420
YouTube doesn't call them TrueView ads for nothing.

00:13:14.420 --> 00:13:17.380
You have to watch them in order to watch the video that you came for.

00:13:17.380 --> 00:13:19.860
And even if you did wait the five seconds and then click "skip",

00:13:19.860 --> 00:13:23.400
well, that's just the same as you tuning out the ad on TV and walking to get more chips.

00:13:23.400 --> 00:13:26.980
But at least in this case, I know that I got five solid seconds with you.

00:13:26.980 --> 00:13:28.600
And honestly, the benefits keep coming!

00:13:28.600 --> 00:13:32.200
Because of online data, I'm able to see the direct impact of my ad.

00:13:32.200 --> 00:13:34.360
Did you click "skip" or did you not?

00:13:34.360 --> 00:13:36.700
Did you click through to my website, or did you not?

00:13:36.700 --> 00:13:40.160
So instead of spending the five million dollars all at once and crossing my fingers

00:13:40.160 --> 00:13:42.620
that you like it, and that it works, and that you go to my website,

00:13:42.620 --> 00:13:46.140
and that you download the thing, and that you buy the app, or whatever the F it is,

00:13:46.140 --> 00:13:48.140
I can instead run the ad on a small scale,

00:13:48.140 --> 00:13:50.620
hone it and re-cut it to make it better and more effective,

00:13:50.620 --> 00:13:53.100
and then scale it up to that five million dollars!

00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:55.600
The money is going to be more effective,

00:13:55.600 --> 00:13:59.400
meaning that my risk as a business owner is drastically reduced.

00:13:59.400 --> 00:14:02.020
So for the same price, I'm getting just as many views,

00:14:02.020 --> 00:14:04.600
with more reliability in the eyeballs that are watching me,

00:14:04.600 --> 00:14:06.560
and also HOW they're watching me.

00:14:06.560 --> 00:14:09.500
This is all good stuff, and it doesn't stop there.

00:14:09.500 --> 00:14:13.340
An ad like this would have a greater chance at virality and more staying power.

00:14:13.340 --> 00:14:17.000
Need an example? Look no further than the one ad that keeps kicking your ass,

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:19.440
(Is it the Legend27?) The Legend27.

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:22.600
By slamming YouTube with this ad everywhere for a month,

00:14:22.600 --> 00:14:25.420
Game of War was able to drill the epic lore of the Legend27

00:14:25.420 --> 00:14:27.900
into enough people's heads that it created a meme,

00:14:27.900 --> 00:14:30.960
that has kept people talking about it weeks after it stopped being funny,

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:33.400
creating their own spin-offs and remix videos.

00:14:33.400 --> 00:14:36.740
It's also weeks after they stopped running it as an ad unit.

00:14:36.740 --> 00:14:40.640
That, my friends, is the Holy Grail of advertising,

00:14:40.640 --> 00:14:44.600
resulting in not just more views, but the sort of cultural impact that

00:14:44.600 --> 00:14:49.340
"Wasuuuuuuuuup!" and "Where's the beef?" videos have had off of the Super Bowl in the past.

00:14:49.340 --> 00:14:53.200
It's not a one or two day spike like Doritos sees from advertising on the Super Bowl,

00:14:53.200 --> 00:14:57.240
it is a multi-week to multi-month to lifelong change

00:14:57.240 --> 00:15:00.200
in the way that people speak and behave around your product.

00:15:00.200 --> 00:15:02.600
Heck, you could even throw some YouTubers into the mix!

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:07.860
I guarantee that they cost a whole lot less than the 500,000 to 2 million dollars

00:15:07.860 --> 00:15:11.560
traditional celebrities charge for their 30 seconds of ad spots.

00:15:11.560 --> 00:15:14.000
Heck, I bet most would do it for the free bag of Doritos,

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:16.320
or an awesome selfie with puppy-monkey-baby.

00:15:16.320 --> 00:15:18.820
That stuff is social media gold.

00:15:18.820 --> 00:15:22.060
But hey, that's just a theory.

00:15:22.060 --> 00:15:23.820
A film theory!

00:15:23.820 --> 00:15:27.760
Aaaaaaaand touchdown!

